  "editorial": {
    "status": "working-edition",
    "notes": "The prayer that asks an angel to carry the offering to the altar on high, said deeply bowed. QUOTQUOT at w024 is indeclinable for case and gender though plural in itself; the nominative is the one the sentence requires, as the subject of sumpsérimus, and no gender is claimed, since nothing marks one. SUMPSÉRIMUS at w035 is read as future perfect rather than perfect subjunctive — the two are spelled alike, and a clause inside a purpose clause can be drawn into the subjunctive with it: the generalizing relative clause quotquot ... sumpsérimus sits inside the ut clause whose one verb is repleámur, and the sense — as many as shall have received — settles the indicative with the received renderings. OSCULATUR ALTARE at s03 falls between quotquot and ex hac, at the mention of this altar, where witness do and the Tridentine books print it. FÍLII at w030 is the genitive singular governed by Corpus et Sánguinem, and Collatinus reads it so; Whitaker's contracted-genitive entry for filius is the whole of the disagreement, and it is recorded in the pipeline's rulings. The close Per eúndem follows a naming of the Son by title only — Fílii tui — the one place where the rule that eúndem answers a naming needs its wider reading. PERFÉRRI at w008 is a compound of fero and carries no conjugation. ÁNGELI at w012 is singular — one angel, unnamed. The comma the edition prints after ut, with witnesses do and mr, marks off the whole clause that quotquot opens.",
